Feature: Conversion of Spark SQL logic for supplier invoice fact table to PySpark with Unity Catalog integration

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog is set to "purgo_databricks"
    And the Unity Catalog schema is "purgo_playground"
    And all required input parameters are provided via dbutils.widgets
    And reusable functions and constants are imported from DA_CCG_RSD_EU
    And the Spark session is configured for dynamic partition overwrite
    And the control table is read using the provided config_unity_catalog and cl_control_table
    And the job context is set using jobId logic (with fallback if not triggered by dbutils.notebook.run)
    And all referenced tables exist and are accessible in the Unity Catalog

  Scenario Outline: Successful conversion of SQL logic to PySpark DataFrame
    Given the following input parameters:
      | target_table_path | partition_key | table_format | compression | table_name | unity_catalog | environment | project | load_type | view_unity_catalog_name | raw_unity_catalog | raw_unity_catalog_hist | config_unity_catalog | edp_lkp_unity_catalog | dims_unity_catalog |
      | <target_table_path> | <partition_key> | <table_format> | <compression> | <table_name> | <unity_catalog> | <environment> | <project> | <load_type> | <view_unity_catalog_name> | <raw_unity_catalog> | <raw_unity_catalog_hist> | <config_unity_catalog> | <edp_lkp_unity_catalog> | <dims_unity_catalog> |
    And all required functions (e.g., get_basejob_url, read_control_table) are available
    When the PySpark code reads and transforms the following source tables:
      | Table Name                        | Source Catalog         |
      | dw_ap_sla_aging_invoice_ca        | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_ap_sla_expense_dist_cf         | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_party_d                        | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_supplier_site_d                | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_internal_org_d_tl              | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_ap_terms_d_tl                  | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_natural_account_d              | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_ap_sla_payments_cf             | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_gl_segment_d_tl                | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | dw_gl_code_combination_d          | <raw_unity_catalog>    |
      | edp_lkup                          | <edp_lkp_unity_catalog>|
      | dim_wf_company                    | <dims_unity_catalog>   |
    And the following transformations are applied:
      | Step                                                                                 | Description                                                                                   |
      | Window function for latest snapshot per invoice                                      | Use RowNumber partitioned by invoice_id, ordered by snapshot_captured_date DESC               |
      | Window function for latest expense dist per distribution                             | Use RowNumber partitioned by multiple keys, ordered by xla_manual_override_flag DESC          |
      | Join logic as per SQL (LEFT JOINs, COALESCE, CASE, etc.)                             | All joins and logic are replicated in PySpark using DataFrame API                             |
      | Filtering by invoice_accounting_date, invoice_source_code, supplier_number, segments | All WHERE clause logic is implemented in PySpark                                              |
      | Exclusion of specific concat_segments and supplier_numbers                           | Use .filter() and .isin()/.notin() as appropriate                                             |
      | Null-safe logic for division code and other fields                                   | Use PySpark's when/otherwise and coalesce                                                     |
      | All column casts and formatting (date_format, cast, concat_ws, etc.)                 | Use PySpark functions for type conversion and formatting                                      |
    And the output DataFrame contains all required columns as per the SQL select
    And the DataFrame is written to the target table path in the specified format and compression, partitioned by the partition key
    Then the output table/view "<unity_catalog>.<table_name>" is created or overwritten successfully
    And the job metadata (jobId, jobUrl) is logged for traceability

    Examples:
      | target_table_path | partition_key | table_format | compression | table_name         | unity_catalog     | environment | project | load_type | view_unity_catalog_name | raw_unity_catalog | raw_unity_catalog_hist | config_unity_catalog | edp_lkp_unity_catalog | dims_unity_catalog |
      | /mnt/fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | invoice_accounting_date | delta | snappy | fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | purgo_playground | prod | CCG | full | vw_fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | purgo_raw | purgo_raw_hist | purgo_config | purgo_edp_lkp | purgo_dims |
      | /mnt/fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | invoice_accounting_date | parquet | gzip | fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | purgo_playground | dev | CCG | incremental | vw_fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin | purgo_raw | purgo_raw_hist | purgo_config | purgo_edp_lkp | purgo_dims |

  Scenario Outline: Error when required input parameter is missing
    Given the input parameter "<missing_param>" is not provided
    When the PySpark code attempts to run
    Then an error is raised with message "Missing required parameter: <missing_param>"

    Examples:
      | missing_param              |
      | target_table_path          |
      | partition_key              |
      | table_format               |
      | compression                |
      | table_name                 |
      | unity_catalog              |
      | environment                |
      | project                    |
      | load_type                  |
      | view_unity_catalog_name    |
      | raw_unity_catalog          |
      | raw_unity_catalog_hist     |
      | config_unity_catalog       |
      | edp_lkp_unity_catalog      |
      | dims_unity_catalog         |

  Scenario Outline: Error when input parameter type is invalid
    Given the input parameter "<param>" is provided with value "<invalid_value>"
    When the PySpark code validates input types
    Then an error is raised with message "Invalid value for <param>: <invalid_value>"

    Examples:
      | param              | invalid_value      |
      | partition_key      | 123                |
      | table_format       | "unsupported_fmt"  |
      | compression        | "none"             |
      | unity_catalog      | ""                 |
      | table_name         | ""                 |

  Scenario Outline: Error when referenced table does not exist or is inaccessible
    Given the table "<table_name>" in catalog "<catalog>" does not exist or is inaccessible
    When the PySpark code attempts to read the table
    Then an error is raised with message "Table <catalog>.<table_name> not found or inaccessible"

    Examples:
      | table_name                  | catalog                |
      | dw_ap_sla_aging_invoice_ca  | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_ap_sla_expense_dist_cf   | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_party_d                  | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_supplier_site_d          | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_internal_org_d_tl        | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_ap_terms_d_tl            | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_natural_account_d        | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_ap_sla_payments_cf       | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_gl_segment_d_tl          | purgo_raw              |
      | dw_gl_code_combination_d    | purgo_raw              |
      | edp_lkup                    | purgo_edp_lkp          |
      | dim_wf_company              | purgo_dims             |

  Scenario: Error when required function is missing
    Given the function "read_control_table" is not available
    When the PySpark code attempts to call the function
    Then an error is raised with message "Function read_control_table not found"

  Scenario: Error when required constant is missing
    Given the constant "cl_control_table" is not defined
    When the PySpark code attempts to use the constant
    Then an error is raised with message "Constant cl_control_table not defined"

  Scenario Outline: Error when duplicate records are found for upsert key
    Given the upsert key "<upsert_key>" has duplicate records in the source data
    When the PySpark code attempts to write to the target table
    Then an error is raised with message "Duplicate records found for upsert key: <upsert_key>"

    Examples:
      | upsert_key                       |
      | invoice_id                       |
      | (invoice_distribution_id, invoice_id) |

  Scenario Outline: Error when type mismatch occurs in transformation
    Given the column "<column>" has value "<invalid_value>" which does not match expected type "<expected_type>"
    When the PySpark code attempts to cast or transform the column
    Then an error is raised with message "Type mismatch for column <column>: expected <expected_type>, got <invalid_value>"

    Examples:
      | column                  | invalid_value | expected_type |
      | transaction_amount      | "abc"         | double        |
      | invoice_accounting_date | "notadate"    | date          |
      | supplier_number         | null          | string        |

  Scenario: Error when business logic for unfinished CTE is required
    Given the SQL code contains an unfinished or commented-out CTE (e.g., P05)
    When the PySpark code attempts to convert the logic
    Then an error is raised with message "Business logic for CTE P05 is incomplete or missing"

  Scenario: Error when output format or destination is not specified
    Given the output format or destination is not provided
    When the PySpark code attempts to write the DataFrame
    Then an error is raised with message "Output format or destination not specified"

  Scenario Outline: Successful validation of allowed values for input parameters
    Given the input parameter "<param>" is provided with value "<allowed_value>"
    When the PySpark code validates allowed values
    Then the value is accepted and processing continues

    Examples:
      | param         | allowed_value |
      | table_format  | delta         |
      | table_format  | parquet       |
      | compression   | snappy        |
      | compression   | gzip          |

  Scenario Outline: Error when input parameter value is not allowed
    Given the input parameter "<param>" is provided with value "<not_allowed_value>"
    When the PySpark code validates allowed values
    Then an error is raised with message "Value <not_allowed_value> not allowed for <param>"

    Examples:
      | param         | not_allowed_value |
      | table_format  | csv              |
      | compression   | lz4              |

# END